# The trust store this repository ships, RECORDED: the ca-certificates version
# that produced it, and a digest over the anchor set itself.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (PLAN-036 decision (h)). os/build-env/images.env pins the
# Debian base by digest and says at its own head that such a pin covers the base
# image's filesystem and NOT the apt archive installed on top of it. So the store
# ca-certificates-generate.sh builds is a function of WHEN it was built as well
# as of this tree: deb.debian.org carries one version per package in a suite and
# re-points it on every point release. For most payloads that drift is absorbed
# and shows as a rebuilt file. This one is the set of certificate authorities
# every device will believe, and nothing else in the tree would notice it
# changing -- the assertions around it are counts, not pinned sets, and the
# package gate's two builds are minutes apart on one mirror state.
#
# NOT A PIN, and that difference is the design. apt still installs whatever
# trixie carries, so a distrusted authority still LEAVES the store on the next
# rebuild; pinning the version would have held the fleet on a stale set, which
# for a trust store is the worse of the two failures. What is recorded here is
# what arrived last time, so a change is refused by name instead of absorbed.
#
# Plain `KEY=value` lines only: no logic, no expansion, no command substitution.
# ca-certificates-generate.sh sources this file from beside itself.
#
# THE REFRESH PROCEDURE, and it is deliberately manual. When the build refuses it
# prints each recorded value beside the one that arrived. Re-record BOTH -- they
# move together, because a new ca-certificates is what changes the anchors -- in
# ONE commit that changes nothing else. THE DIFF IS THE REVIEW: two lines
# changing here is a reviewer being told the fleet's trust store moved, and that
# is the whole mechanism. Do not fold a refresh into an unrelated change, and do
# not update one value while leaving the other.

# `dpkg-query -W -f='${Version}' ca-certificates`, read in the generate stage
# while the package is still installed. By the time
# ca-certificates-verify.sh runs it has been purged, which is why the guard
# cannot live there.
CA_CERTIFICATES_VERSION=20250419

# sha256 over the `sha256sum` of every regular file under
# /usr/share/ca-certificates, NUL-sorted by path and hashed as one stream. It
# covers the anchors' CONTENTS and their NAMES, so one anchor added, removed,
# renamed or edited moves it. NUL-sorted because one anchor's name is not ASCII
# (NetLock_Arany_=Class_Gold=_Fotanusitvany).
CA_CERTIFICATES_ANCHORS_SHA256=f98a1cbca4003c3b4d97248c94a8a217f6e4c3d9f86397bd7725e61c1ec67bd6
